artifact.self_contained_problem:
Within general relativity, a naked singularity is a spacetime singularity that is not hidden behind an event horizon, in contrast to a classical (Kerr-type) astrophysical black hole. Consider a compact gravitating object that could be modeled either as a Kerr (or Schwarzschild) black hole or as a naked singularity solution of the Einstein field equations (for example, of Janis–Newman–Winicour, Joshi–Malafarina–Narayan, or Brans–Dicke–Kerr type). 

Develop a set of theoretical observational tests, suitable for multimessenger observations of astrophysical compact objects, that can in principle distinguish a naked singularity from an astrophysical black hole. Specifically, determine for each of the following classes of signatures whether—and under what precise conditions on the spacetime parameters—a measurable deviation exists between the two cases:

(i) the geometry of the neighbourhood of the object (e.g. existence and location of photon spheres, ISCO, frame-dragging angular velocity);
(ii) the spectrum and flux of energetic photons emerging from the high-density central region;
(iii) deviations from classical timelike and null geodesic motion of test particles;
(iv) the luminosity, temperature profile, and integrated flux of a thin accretion disk;
(v) the structure of photon rings and the shape and size of the central shadow in resolved images;
(vi) the energy spectrum of products of high-energy particle collisions in the strong-field region;
(vii) high-frequency (kHz and above) gravitational-wave signatures during inspiral, merger, and ringdown.

The problem is to determine whether the combined set of observables (i)–(vii), taken in realistic multimessenger observation scenarios, suffices to unambiguously discriminate naked singularities from black holes of comparable mass and spin.

